    "engine": "jupyter",
    "markdown": "---\ngd-head-tags:\n  - >-\n    `<script type=\"application/ld+json\">[{\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"SoftwareSourceCode\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\", \"description\":\n    \"High-performance graph analysis library\", \"codeRepository\":\n    \"https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase\", \"license\":\n    \"https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT\", \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"documentation\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase\", \"programmingLanguage\": {\"@type\":\n    \"ComputerLanguage\", \"name\": \"Python\"}}, {\"@context\": \"https://schema.org\",\n    \"@type\": \"WebPage\", \"name\": \"Graph.is_forest - EdgeCase\", \"isPartOf\":\n    {\"@type\": \"WebSite\", \"name\": \"EdgeCase\"}, \"url\":\n    \"https://rich-iannone.github.io/edgecase/reference/Graph.is_forest.html\"}]</script>`{=html}\nopen-graph:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.is_forest()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Check if the graph is a forest.\ntwitter-card:\n  title: >-\n    [Graph.is_forest()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n    .doc-label-method} | EdgeCase\n  description: Check if the graph is a forest.\ntitle: >-\n  [Graph.is_forest()]{.doc-object-name .doc-function .doc-label\n  .doc-label-method}\nbody-classes: doc-api-page doc-py-api-page\nshift-heading-level-by: 0\nbread-crumbs: false\ntoc-depth: 3\ndescription-meta: Check if the graph is a forest.\npage-navigation: false\nhtml-table-processing: none\n---\n\n::: {.doc-subject}\n\nCheck if the graph is a forest.\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-usage-source}\nUsage\n\n[Source](https://github.com/rich-iannone/edgecase/blob/main/python/edgecase/graph.py#L25273-L25359){target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\"}\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-signature .doc-function}\n```pythonsignature\nGraph.is_forest()\n```\n:::\n\n::: {.doc-text}\n\n![Is forest](../assets/graphs/is-forest.light.svg){.lightbox fig-alt=\"Left: two separate trees, one on A, B, D, E and one on C and F; neither has a cycle, so is_forest() returns True. Right: the same two trees plus an edge between D and E, which closes the triangle B, D, E (shown in the accent color), so is_forest() returns False.\" width=\"100%\"}\n\nA forest is an acyclic, undirected graph (disjoint union of trees). For a graph with *n*\nnodes and *k* connected components to be a forest it:\n\n- must be undirected\n- must have exactly *n - k* edges\n- must be acyclic\n\nThe method counts the connected components and compares the edge count with *n - k*;\nany surplus edge necessarily closes a cycle. Unlike `is_tree()`, a forest may consist of\nseveral separate pieces, and isolated nodes count as one-node trees. Typical uses:\n\n- checking that a graph has no cycles at all, regardless of how many components it has\n- validating collections of hierarchies (e.g. several family trees or taxonomies)\n- confirming that an edge-removal or spanning-forest step left no redundant links\n\nSelf-loops and parallel edges are cycles, so they make the result `False`. Directed\ngraphs always return `False` (except the empty graph); for directed acyclicity use\n`is_dag()`. The whole graph is checked and the current selection is ignored.\n:::\n\n## Returns {.doc-returns}\n\n::: {.doc-definition-items}\n<code> [bool]{.doc-parameter-annotation}</code>\n\n:   `True` if graph is a forest, `False` otherwise. An empty graph is a forest.\n:::\n\n## Technical Notes {.doc-technical-notes}\n\nA forest is exactly the disjoint union of trees, so a single tree is a forest with\n$k = 1$, and the empty graph is a forest with $k = 0$. The edge-count law $|E| = n - k$\ndrops straight out of applying $|E| = n_i - 1$ to each of the $k$ component trees and\nsumming over $i$; combined with acyclicity this is what the check actually verifies.\n\n## See Also {.doc-see-also}\n\n[](`~is_tree`) \n\n:   Whether the graph is a single tree.\n\n[](`~is_branching`) \n\n:   The directed version of a forest.\n\n[](`~weakly_connected_components`) \n\n:   List the connected pieces of the graph.\n\n## Examples {.doc-examples}\n\nUnlike `is_tree()`, this doesn't require one connected component. Several disjoint trees\ntogether form a forest, and so does a single tree. A cycle is neither:\n\n::: {#88f04d24 .cell execution_count=1}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\nimport edgecase as ec\n\nforest = ec.Graph.from_edges([(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"C\", \"D\")])\ncycle = ec.Graph.from_edges([(\"A\", \"B\"), (\"B\", \"C\"), (\"C\", \"A\")])\n\nforest.is_forest(), forest.is_tree(), cycle.is_forest()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=1}\n```\n(True, False, False)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nA directed graph is never reported as a forest, even if its shape would qualify; use\n`is_dag()` to check a directed graph for cycles:\n\n::: {#80618b8c .cell execution_count=2}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\ndirected = ec.Graph(directed=True).add_path_graph(n=3)\n\ndirected.is_forest(), directed.is_dag()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=2}\n```\n(False, True)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\n### A company without its head\n\nA company's reporting lines, without directions, are a tree. Taking out the chief executive,\nMira, leaves the four departments that reported to her as four separate trees, which\ntogether are a forest:\n\n::: {#307233fb .cell execution_count=3}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\norg = ec.org_chart().to_undirected().delete_node(\"Mira\")\n\norg.is_tree(), org.is_forest(), org.count_weakly_connected_components()\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=3}\n```\n(False, True, 4)\n```\n:::\n:::\n\n\nThe four trees, colored by department:\n\n::: {#478adfbf .cell execution_count=4}\n``` {.python .cell-code}\norg.plot(\n    layout=\"pos\", color_by=\"dept\", label_by=\"label\", width=820, height=640\n)\n```\n\n::: {.cell-output .cell-output-display execution_count=4}\n```{=html}\n<div class=\"ec-theme\" style=\"display:contents\"><style class=